declare cid uuid;
begin
  select id into cid from public.courses where slug = 'bib-102';
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 18, $j$5.2 James: Faith That Works$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "James 1 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/James/1"}, {"title": "James 2 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/James/2"}, {"title": "James 3 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/James/3"}, {"title": "James 4 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/James/4"}], "body": "Read James 1, James 2, James 3 and James 4 in the King James Version. Then read this lesson.\n\nThe letter of James was written by James, the brother of Jesus and a leader of the church in Jerusalem, to Jewish believers scattered abroad. It is a very practical book, and it is full of short commands. James taught that trials test our faith and produce patience, and that anyone who lacks wisdom may ask God. He taught that believers must be doers of the word and not hearers only. In chapter 2 he wrote that faith without works is dead, which means that real faith shows itself in action. In chapter 3 he warned about the power of the tongue, and in chapter 4 he urged believers to submit to God, resist the devil and humble themselves.\n\nKey words: trials, patience, wisdom, doers, tongue.\n\nYour task: (1) Tell who James was and who he wrote to. (2) Write James 1:22 in your own words. (3) Explain what James meant when he said that faith without works is dead.\n\nCourse area: Bible elective. Scripture is read from the King James Version.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "Bible elective", "script": "Our next book is James, and it may be the most practical book in the New Testament. The writer is James, the brother of Jesus, who became a leader of the church in Jerusalem. At first James did not believe in Jesus during His earthly ministry, but after the resurrection he became a faithful servant. He wrote this letter to Jewish believers who were scattered and facing hard times.\n\nJames begins with trials. James 1:2 says, My brethren, count it all joy when ye fall into divers temptations; The word temptations here means trials. Verse 3 says, Knowing this, that the trying of your faith worketh patience. And verse 4 says, But let patience have her perfect work, that ye may be perfect and entire, wanting nothing. God uses hard times to grow us. James also gave an invitation in verse 5, If any of you lack wisdom, let him ask of God, that giveth to all men liberally, and upbraideth not; and it shall be given him. Students, if you need wisdom for a test, a friendship or a decision, you can ask God.\n\nThen James gave a key command. James 1:22 says, But be ye doers of the word, and not hearers only, deceiving your own selves. It is not enough to listen to a sermon or read a Bible verse. We must do what it says.\n\nIn chapter 2 James explained that real faith shows itself in action. James 2:14 says, What doth it profit, my brethren, though a man say he hath faith, and have not works? can faith save him? He did not say that works save us. Paul had already made clear that we are saved by grace through faith. James said that true faith produces good works, just as a living body shows signs of life. James 2:26 says, For as the body without the spirit is dead, so faith without works is dead also. Paul and James do not disagree. Paul teaches how we are saved, and James teaches what saving faith looks like.\n\nChapter 3 is about the tongue. James 3:5 says, Even so the tongue is a little member, and boasteth great things. Behold, how great a matter a little fire kindleth! A few careless words can hurt many people, so we must guard our speech.\n\nChapter 4 ends with a call to draw near to God. James 4:7 says, Submit yourselves therefore to God. Resist the devil, and he will flee from you. Verse 8 says, Draw nigh to God, and he will draw nigh to you. And verse 10 says, Humble yourselves in the sight of the Lord, and he shall lift you up.\n\nYour task is to tell who James was and who he wrote to, write James 1:22 in your own words, and explain what James meant when he said that faith without works is dead. Then take the four question check.\n\nReal faith is a faith that lives, and it shows in what we do. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$5.2 James: Faith That Works$j$);
end
$seed$;
